   231	The Compose project is named `exposed`. When testing a frontend-only change in a
   232	worktree against an already running API, use
   233	`docker compose up --build -d --no-deps frontend` to avoid recreating the backend
   234	and database from that worktree. This serves the frontend from the worktree.
   235	
   236	### Frontend development and checks
   237	
   238	For Docker Compose, configure PostHog in the repository root `.env`. Copy
   239	`.env.example` if the file does not exist, or add its `PUBLIC_POSTHOG_*` entries
   240	to your existing `.env`. Replace the token placeholder with your PostHog project
   241	token and set `PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST` to your project's ingestion host. The example
   242	uses the EU host, `https://eu.i.posthog.com`.
   243	
   244	Compose passes these values to the frontend container. They take precedence over
   245	`frontend/.env`, including when the root values are empty. After changing the
   246	root `.env`, recreate the frontend from the repository root:
   247	
   248	```sh
   249	docker compose up -d --no-deps frontend
   250	```
   251	
   252	Both values are public browser configuration. Development raises a client error
   253	if either value is missing.
   254	
   255	To run the frontend on the host instead of Docker, use Node.js **24+**:
   256	
   257	```sh
   258	cd frontend
   259	test -f .env || cp .env.example .env
   260	npm ci
   261	```
   262	
   263	Set the same PostHog values in `frontend/.env`, then start the development server:
   264	
   265	```sh
   266	npm run dev
   267	```
   268	
   269	The frontend server reads `http://127.0.0.1:6999` by default. Set the private
   270	`EXPOSED_API_ORIGIN` environment variable to select another backend. For the
   271	Compose backend, run the frontend inside Compose because the Rust port is private.
   272	Browser requests never call the Rust API directly.
   273	
   274	`npm run build` produces an adapter-node server in `frontend/build`. Run it with
   275	`EXPOSED_API_ORIGIN=http://127.0.0.1:6999 node build` from `frontend`.
   276	Set `HOST` and `PORT` for the listening address. The `frontend-server` target in
   277	`docker/Dockerfile.frontend` runs only the Node frontend on port 8080. Deploy the
   278	Rust server separately and set the frontend's private `EXPOSED_API_ORIGIN` to its
   279	reachable origin. The frontend image does not require `DATABASE_URL`.
   280	
   281	Search URLs preserve only the query, for example `/?q=John`. Search strictness is
   282	application configuration, set to `0.8` in
   283	[`frontend/src/lib/search.ts`](frontend/src/lib/search.ts), alongside the search
   284	delay and result limits. Legacy URLs with `strictness` redirect to the same page
   285	without that parameter. The frontend always sends its configured strictness to
   286	the API. The API caps each entity type separately, so the UI describes the returned
   287	results without claiming a total count or offering unsupported pagination.
   288	The API also defaults to `0.8` when `strictness` is omitted. Explicit thresholds
   289	control primary trigram matches and corrected fallback similarity. When primary
   290	matches contain no MP or Individual funder,
   291	search appends phonetic person matches after the primary results. Organization
   292	matches do not suppress this fallback. Each query word is corrected against
   293	each candidate independently, choosing the
   294	closest name word with the same Soundex code and at most one edit. Name words
   295	can be reused. Every query word must match, and the corrected query must meet
   296	the requested `word_similarity` threshold against that candidate. Extra name
   297	words are allowed; words from different aliases cannot combine. Fallback
   298	results rank by total edits, then corrected similarity and stable identity.
   299	Canonical names win alias ties. Primary and fallback funders share the per-type result limit.
   300	
   301	In-app feedback collection is future work. Feedback is gathered through manual
   302	testing.
